## Reached at
  A live HTTP service on port 3333. Both caller suites send plain HTTP GET
  requests to `http://localhost:3333`; the calling shapes are
      requests.get("http://localhost:3333/<path>", timeout=10)
  and
      conn = http.client.HTTPConnection("localhost", 3333, timeout=10)
      conn.request("GET", path)
  The service answering that port is the project-owned Go binary
  `/app/webp-server`, built from the Go module checked out at `/app`
  (module `webp_server_go`):
      go build -ldflags="-s -w" -o webp-server .
  The container entrypoint, in a restart loop, executes
      ./webp-server --config=/app/config.json
  with working directory `/app`. Nothing else listens on that port and no
  proxy sits in front of it, so every request lands in this binary. The
  module source is staged into `/app` by the image build, and the binary the
  entrypoint runs is only the one at `/app/webp-server` — a binary not
  rebuilt from the `/app` source keeps answering in its place.

## Must provide
  HTTP listener            bound to HOST:PORT taken from the JSON config file
                           (`0.0.0.0` and `3333` in /app/config.json)
  catch-all GET route      a single GET route covering every request path —
                           registered on the fiber app in the main package as
                           `app.Get("/*", ...)`; whatever the path, each GET is
                           handled by project-owned code
  `--config` CLI flag      names the JSON config file (default `config.json`);
                           keys honored: HOST, PORT, QUALITY (string-encoded
                           number), IMG_PATH, EXHAUST_PATH, ALLOWED_TYPES (a
                           list of extensions), ENABLE_AVIF
  percent-encoded paths    callers address files by percent-encoded request
                           paths; a file with a non-ASCII name is requested
                           only through its percent-encoded form, so a
                           percent-encoded request-target must resolve to the
                           on-disk file it names
  image-root resolution    request paths resolve against IMG_PATH (`./pics`)
                           with cache output under EXHAUST_PATH (`./exhaust`),
                           both relative to the working directory `/app`
  rebuilt artifact         `/app/webp-server` rebuilt from the `/app` module
                           source; the entrypoint executes exactly this path

## Called as
  Every call is one shape: a GET on the catch-all route.
  path     1st and only request input — the request-target, a string. Callers
           pass a bare filename at the image root (`/webp_server.jpg`,
           `/big.jpg`), a path one subdirectory deep (`/dir1/inside.jpg`), or
           a percent-encoded filename (`/%E5%A4%AA%E7%A5%9E%E5%95%A6.png`).
           The second caller passes request-targets carrying further
           percent-encoded sequences, delivered byte-for-byte by its HTTP
           client.
  headers  optional; one call sends
               Accept: image/webp,image/apng,image/*,*/*;q=0.8
               User-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; WebP-capable browser)

## Setup required
  - The listener must be up on port 3333 before callers proceed: both poll
    with GET requests until the service answers.
  - The service runs with working directory `/app`, because IMG_PATH `./pics`
    and EXHAUST_PATH `./exhaust` are relative. `/app/pics` (fixture images,
    including one inside `dir1/` and one with a non-ASCII name),
    `/app/exhaust` (cache directory), and `/app/config.json` are staged by
    the image build; they are resources, not implementation.
  - Build chain in `/app`: `go mod download`, then
    `go build -ldflags="-s -w" -o webp-server .`. go.mod pins the fiber
    router and the webp encoder to forked modules via `replace` directives;
    the Go toolchain and these modules are dependencies, not project-owned
    code. If the process exits, the entrypoint restarts it, and the callers'"'"'
    readiness polling rides through that.

## Test harness boundary
  One caller is a pytest process that issues GETs through the `requests`
  library and polls the service at startup. The second caller issues GETs
  through Python'"'"'s stdlib `http.client`, chosen because it transmits the
  request-target exactly as given, percent-encoded sequences included,
  without rewriting it; that transport choice belongs to the calling side.
  A runner script installs pytest and requests under `uv` before running.
  The runner, the polling loops, and both HTTP clients are the calling side,
  not the implementation — do not rebuild them.
```

