## Called as
  Pipeline objects are constructed with a caller-supplied downstream Pipeline
  and driven through the base-class interface:
      Pl_ASCII85Decoder dec("a85", &sink);
      dec.write(buf, len);
      dec.finish();
  identifier — 1st constructor argument, a char const* string; next — 2nd
  constructor argument, a pointer to a Pipeline object owned by the caller;
  write() — byte buffer passed as (unsigned char*, size_t), one or more
  calls; then finish(). Pl_ASCIIHexDecoder is called with the same shape.

  Pl_AES_PDF is constructed with, in order: an identifier string, a
  downstream Pipeline pointer, a direction flag (bool), key bytes
  (unsigned char const*) and their length (size_t). The caller then invokes
  useZeroIV() and disablePadding() before writing buffers through write()
  and finish(). Callers construct separate objects for each direction and
  for each phase of a run, and also continue calling write() and finish()
  on an existing object across phases.

  Pl_Count is called with the same identifier/next shape; the caller reads
  getCount() and getLastChar() between phases and reuses the same object —
  further write() calls and finish() on the object after a previous
  finish().

  The CLI is invoked as /usr/local/bin/qpdf with an argument list of
  strings; the positional arguments are filesystem paths naming an input
  file and an output file:
      qpdf --decode-level=all --compress-streams=n <in.pdf> <out.pdf>
      qpdf --normalize-content=y <in.pdf> <out.pdf>
      qpdf --encrypt <user-password> <owner-password> <key-length> -- <in.pdf> <out.pdf>
      qpdf --password=<password> --decode-level=all --compress-streams=n <in.pdf> <out.pdf>
      qpdf <in.pdf> <out.pdf>
  <user-password> and <owner-password> — strings; <key-length> — a number;
  file paths — strings naming files on disk. Input files are written by the
  caller before the call: either a small PDF it assembles itself (whose
  content stream it encodes with its own reference codec) or the binary
  corpus fixture file. The output path names a file the caller reads after
  the call.

