    OllamaLLMModelPref   ->  OLLAMA_MODEL_PREF
    LocalAiApiKey        ->  LOCAL_AI_API_KEY
    AuthToken            ->  AUTH_TOKEN   (set and cleared through /api/system/update-password)
    JWTSecret            ->  JWT_SECRET   (set and cleared through /api/system/update-password)

  Artifact path:
    /app/server/.env     the configuration file the server writes and the callers read

## Called as
  POST /api/system/update-env
      JSON body: a single-entry object mapping one public body key to a string value:
          { "OllamaLLMModelPref": <string> }   or   { "LocalAiApiKey": <string> }
      1st (body) input — the value at the body key; a string.
      Optional header: `Authorization: Bearer <string>`.

  GET /api/env-dump
      No inputs.

  GET /api/v1/system/env-dump
      No inputs.

  POST /api/system/update-password
      JSON body:
          { "usePassword": <boolean>, "newPassword": <string> }
      2nd body property — `newPassword`; a string, present when `usePassword` is true,
      absent when `usePassword` is false.
      Optional header: `Authorization: Bearer <string>`.

  POST /api/request-token
      JSON body:
          { "password": <string> }
      1st body property — `password`; a string.

  GET /api/ping
      No inputs; polled repeatedly until the service answers.

  dumpENV() — module surface
      Called with no arguments. Its input arrives through the environment of the
      process that loaded the module: the calling subprocess first assigns
      `process.env.OLLAMA_MODEL_PREF = <string>` (the string is the subprocess'"'"'s first
      CLI argument), then invokes `dumpENV()` on the required module. The call is made
      from a subprocess run with working directory `/app/server`.

  Project-internal callers of the same module (the route handlers above) call:
      updateENV(body, false, response?.locals?.user?.id)     from /api/system/update-env
      updateENV({ AuthToken: <string>, JWTSecret: <string> }, true)
                                                             from /api/system/update-password
      dumpENV()                                              from the env-dump routes and
                                                             from the two update routes
      1st argument — a plain object mapping public body keys to values; 2nd argument —
      a boolean; 3rd argument — an id or undefined.

## Setup required
  - The application server process is already running and listening on port 3001 before
    any request is made. It runs under a supervising restart loop that starts
    `NODE_ENV=production node index.js` from `/app/server`; callers poll `GET /api/ping`
    until it answers, and they kill the process matching `node index.js` to force a
    supervised restart, which reloads `/app/server/.env` at startup.
  - `NODE_ENV` is `production` in the container (set by the image build and by the
    supervisor command). The env-dump routes and the two update routes perform the
    .env write only under `NODE_ENV === "production"`.
  - `/app/server/.env` exists — staged by the image build — and is writable by the
    server process; the module resolves this file relative to its own location two
    directories up (`/app/server/utils/helpers/updateENV.js` -> `/app/server/.env`).
  - For the module surface: a `node` executable on `PATH`; the module must resolve by
    absolute path exactly to `/app/server/utils/helpers/updateENV.js`; the driving
    subprocess runs with working directory `/app/server`.

## Test harness boundary
  - A Python pytest runner with the `requests` library issues all HTTP calls against
    `http://localhost:3001`. Its readiness polling, JSON body construction, response
    parsing, and its own reader/writer for `/app/server/.env` belong to the calling
    side, not to the implementation.
  - A Node driver script is run through `subprocess` as `node <driver script> <value>`
    with working directory `/app/server`; it requires the module by the absolute path
    above, seeds `process.env.OLLAMA_MODEL_PREF` from its CLI argument, and calls
    `dumpENV()`. Its stdout sentinel lines and its exit-code mapping are harness-side
    constructs.
  - The calling side also rewrites `/app/server/.env` directly to reset state and kills
    the server process to trigger the supervisor restart.
  - None of this harness is part of the implementation; the engineer must not rebuild it.
```

