## Reached at
  A compiled binary resolved by absolute path inside the project'"'"'s own build
  tree and invoked through `subprocess`, one fresh caller-created working
  directory per invocation:
      subprocess.run(["/app/bin/gcc/MP4Box", "-dash", "10000", media],
                     cwd=workdir, ...)
      subprocess.run(["/app/bin/gcc/MP4Box", "-info", media],
                     cwd=workdir, ...)
  The binary is a build artifact of the GPAC source tree at `/app`; the image
  build configures that tree with `--static-bin` and builds it with `make`,
  and callers resolve the build output at `/app/bin/gcc/MP4Box` directly. A
  symlink at `/usr/local/bin/MP4Box` exists, but no caller resolves a PATH
  name, so contact lands on the build-tree artifact.
  The same build tree exposes the project'"'"'s static archive, which a
  caller-compiled C program links by absolute path:
      gcc -fsanitize=address -DGPAC_HAVE_CONFIG_H -I/app -I/app/include \
          <driver.c> /app/bin/gcc/libgpac_static.a \
          -lm -lz -lfreetype -lpng -ljpeg -lpthread -ldl -o <driver>
  That driver includes `<gpac/bitstream.h>` and
  `<gpac/internal/isomedia_dev.h>`.

## Must provide
  /app/bin/gcc/MP4Box             executable regular file at this exact
                                  build-tree path
  -dash                           MP4Box option taking one option argument and
                                  an input-file argument
  -info                           MP4Box option taking an input-file argument
  *_dash.mpd, *_dashinit.mp4      artifact paths in the invocation'"'"'s working
                                  directory that callers glob for after a
                                  `-dash` run
  /app/bin/gcc/libgpac_static.a   static archive produced by the same build
  gf_bs_new                       function linked from the archive; yields a
                                  `GF_BitStream *`
  gf_bs_del                       function linked from the archive; takes a
                                  `GF_BitStream *`
  gf_isom_parse_text_sample       function linked from the archive; yields a
                                  `GF_TextSample *`; declared in
                                  `<gpac/internal/isomedia_dev.h>`
  gf_isom_delete_text_sample      function linked from the archive; takes a
                                  `GF_TextSample *`
  <gpac/bitstream.h>              header resolvable under `-I/app/include`;
                                  declares `GF_BitStream`, `gf_bs_new`,
                                  `gf_bs_del`, `GF_BITSTREAM_READ`
  <gpac/internal/isomedia_dev.h>  header resolvable under `-I/app/include`;
                                  declares `GF_TextSample` and the text-sample
                                  functions above
  configuration header            resolvable via `-DGPAC_HAVE_CONFIG_H -I/app`

## Called as
  MP4Box -dash 10000 <file>
      argv[1]   `-dash`
      argv[2]   the option argument; a string
      argv[3]   the input file; a string path
  MP4Box -info <file>
      argv[1]   `-info`
      argv[2]   the input file; a string path
  gf_bs_new(buffer, size, mode)
      buffer  1st argument; a pointer to a caller-owned byte array
      size    2nd argument; the buffer length in bytes
      mode    3rd argument; the constant `GF_BITSTREAM_READ`
  gf_isom_parse_text_sample(bs)
      bs      1st argument; a `GF_BitStream *` opened for reading
  gf_isom_delete_text_sample(s)
      s       1st argument; a `GF_TextSample *`
  gf_bs_del(bs)
      bs      1st argument; a `GF_BitStream *`

## Setup required
  The tree at `/app` must be configured and built by the project'"'"'s own build
  system so that both artifacts exist at exactly `/app/bin/gcc/MP4Box` and
  `/app/bin/gcc/libgpac_static.a`; callers resolve nothing else, so any
  change to the tree must land in those two paths through that build. Callers
  assert the binary is a regular executable file and the archive exists before
  each run.
  Each binary invocation runs in a fresh, empty caller-created temporary
  directory; the input file is a caller-staged copy inside it, and the
  caller'"'"'s environment for the run sets `PATH` to the standard system
  directories and `HOME` and `TMPDIR` to that working directory. No other
  environment state is required, and no global dynamic-linker preload file
  may be active.
  The archive must link into a caller binary compiled with
  `-fsanitize=address -DGPAC_HAVE_CONFIG_H`, with its remaining references
  satisfied by `-lm -lz -lfreetype -lpng -ljpeg -lpthread -ldl` (system
  libraries present in the image).

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner: it copies input files from its own
  fixtures directory (kept alongside the runner) into a fresh temporary
  working directory under randomized file names, invokes the binary by
  absolute path through `subprocess`, and afterwards globs that working
  directory for its output files. The runner, the fixture files, the staging,
  and the globbing belong to the caller, not to the implementation.
  For the library surface, the caller compiles its own single-file C driver
  fresh on every run with AddressSanitizer enabled, links the project'"'"'s
  archive by absolute path, and runs the resulting program with a mode-string
  argument. The driver program itself — its inline byte buffers, its
  liveness-canary mode, and the marker strings it prints — belongs to the
  caller; only the archive, the headers, and the symbols the driver links
  against belong to the implementation.
  A bootstrap script that makes `pytest` importable (from bundled wheels or
  over the network) before a single `python3 -m pytest` invocation is also
  caller-side tooling.
