## Reached at
  A Flask application listening on TCP port 5000 (all interfaces), started by the
  container entrypoint as:
      cd /app && python3 wiki.py
  The application module is /app/wiki.py, staged at that path by the image build;
  at startup it imports config.py, git_manager.py, web_dependencies.py and
  knowledge_graph.py from the same /app directory and renders Jinja2 templates
  from /app/templates/. Every caller reaches it over plain HTTP against
  http://localhost:5000, for example:
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/list/", timeout=10)
  where BASE_URL = "http://localhost:5000". The working directory is /app: the
  config loader (config.py, WikmdConfig) resolves wikmd-config.yaml next to the
  module file and resolves the configured wiki directory name (wiki) relative to
  the process cwd, so the served wiki content lives at /app/wiki.

  Two independent caller suites reach the same routes. The first drives the
  homepage, the page-creation endpoint, both listing routes, the page-serving
  route, the static assets and the two toggle routes. The second suite also lands
  on GET /list/ and GET /list/<path:folderpath>/ and on POST /add_new: it drives
  GET /list/ and GET /list/<path:folderpath>/ with a real subdirectory of the
  wiki, creates a page through POST /add_new, and only afterwards sends further
  GET requests to the parameterized listing route with other folderpath values.
  All of those requests reach the same Flask view functions registered in
  /app/wiki.py.

## Must provide
  GET  /                          homepage route; renders templates/index.html
                                  through templates/base.html
  POST /add_new                   page-creation route; reads form fields PN and CT
  GET  /list/                     full file listing; delegates to the same view as
                                  the parameterized route with an empty folderpath
  GET  /list/<path:folderpath>/   parameterized listing route; one positional
                                  string argument bound through the Flask
                                  <path:...> converter; the view renders
                                  templates/list_files.html with folderpath bound
                                  to the template variable folder
  GET|POST /<path:file_page>      page-serving route; GET renders
                                  templates/content.html for the requested page;
                                  POST dispatches to the search form handler
  GET  /static/<path:filename>    Flask static endpoint backed by /app/static
                                  (favicon.ico is shipped there)
  GET  /toggle-sorting/           listing-sort toggle route
  GET  /toggle-darktheme/         dark-theme toggle route

  Project-owned inputs these routes resolve against: /app/wiki.py holding the
  Flask app object and all route registrations; /app/config.py exposing
  WikmdConfig; /app/git_manager.py exposing WikiRepoManager;
  /app/web_dependencies.py exposing get_web_deps; /app/knowledge_graph.py
  exposing find_links; /app/utils.py; the template set in /app/templates/
  (base.html, index.html, list_files.html, content.html, new.html, search.html,
  login.html, knowledge-graph.html); the static assets in /app/static/; the
  shipped wiki content in /app/wiki/ (including the img/ subdirectory);
  /app/wikmd-config.yaml.

## Called as
  GET /
      requests.get(BASE_URL, timeout=10)
      (the readiness poll issues the same request with timeout=5 in a retry loop
      before any other request is made)

  POST /add_new
      requests.post(BASE_URL + "/add_new",
                    data={"PN": <page name>, "CT": <page body>}, timeout=10)
      PN — form field; a string
      CT — form field; a string
      The caller leaves redirect-following enabled, so the request chain ends at
      GET /<page name>.

  GET /list/
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/list/", timeout=10)

  GET /list/<path:folderpath>/
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/list/img/", timeout=10)
      folderpath — 1st positional argument of the view; a string; the <path:...>
      converter delivers it as a single string that may contain path separators

  GET /<path:file_page>
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/Features", timeout=10)
      file_page — 1st positional argument of the view; a string

  GET /static/<path:filename>
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/static/favicon.ico", timeout=10)

  GET /toggle-sorting/
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/toggle-sorting/", timeout=10,
                   allow_redirects=False)

  GET /toggle-darktheme/
      requests.get(BASE_URL + "/toggle-darktheme/",
                   headers={"Referer": BASE_URL + "/list/"},
                   timeout=10, allow_redirects=False)

  Both toggle callers disable redirect-following and read the redirect target
  from the response themselves.

## Setup required
  - The application process must be up and answering on port 5000 before any
    caller proceeds. The container command is /entrypoint.sh, which changes to
    /app and runs python3 wiki.py in a restart loop. Host and port come from
    /app/wikmd-config.yaml (0.0.0.0:5000).
  - Working directory /app is load-bearing: the config loader resolves
    wikmd-config.yaml relative to the module directory and the wiki directory
    name (wiki) relative to the process cwd.
  - /app/wiki is a git repository with a committed baseline, initialized at image
    build. git_manager.WikiRepoManager opens or creates the repo in the
    configured wiki directory during application startup, and the page-save flow
    spawns a background git-sync thread, so git and the repo metadata must be
    present for page creation to run its normal flow.
  - The pinned runtime dependencies from /app/requirements.txt must be installed
    (Flask 1.1.2, Werkzeug 1.0.1, GitPython, pypandoc, PyYAML and the rest of
    the list); the pandoc binary is installed system-wide. Page conversion
    through pypandoc may fail in this image while the page routes still answer,
    so callers depend on the page frame, not on the converted body.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner inside the container that talks to the
  application only through the requests library over http://localhost:5000. A
  runner script installs pytest if absent, polls the application with curl until
  it answers, and then invokes pytest over the suite directory. The readiness
  poll loop, the runtime page creation used to obtain live listing entries, and
  the caller'"'"'s sequencing of its own listing requests are all caller-side
  machinery. None of that belongs to the implementation; the engineer does not
  rebuild the runner, the poll loop, or the HTTP client code.
